91. PROVE NONEXISTENCE OR UNIQUENESS
Original subquestions: 2. Required results: 2 proofs.

91.a [PASS; required results 1]
Original: Prove (x+5)^2+(y-2)^2=-1 has no solution.
Both squares are nonnegative for all real x,y, so their sum is >=0.
It cannot equal -1. Therefore the real solution set is empty.
This proves the requested nonexistence.

91.b [PASS; required results 1]
Original: Prove (x-7)^2+(y+4)^2=0 has a unique solution.
A sum of two nonnegative real numbers is zero only when both are zero.
Hence x-7=0 and y+4=0, giving x=7,y=-4.
Conversely this pair gives 0^2+0^2=0.
Result: the solution set is exactly {(7,-4)}, proving existence and uniqueness.

GRAPH CONVENTION FOR 92-95
Use Cartesian axes with equal unit lengths when constructing the graphs.
Every graph below specifies the entire solution set, not a sample-only plot.
All listed guide points are to be marked at the stated exact coordinates.
Line constructions extend in both directions without bound.
Hyperbola constructions include every stated branch and no asymptote points.
Positive-radius circle constructions include the entire circumference, not
the disk. Exact parameter ranges are provided as full-set specifications.
No actual image, visual inspection, or rendering is part of this seal.

