93. DESCRIBE AND CONSTRUCT THE GRAPHS
Original subquestions: 2. Required results: 2 descriptions plus 2 graphs.

93.a [PASS; required results 2]
Original: (x-y)(x+y)=0.
By the zero-product property, x-y=0 or x+y=0.
Description: the union of the two complete straight lines y=x and y=-x.
Their intersection is (0,0), which belongs to the graph.
Entire graph:
  {(t,t): t in R} union {(t,-t): t in R}.
Construction: for y=x mark (-1,-1), (0,0), (1,1); for y=-x mark
(-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1). Draw and indefinitely extend both straight lines.
Completeness: either zero factor implies the original product is zero;
every zero product has at least one of those two zero factors.

93.b [PASS; required results 2]
Original: (x-3)(y+1)=0.
By the zero-product property, x=3 or y=-1.
Description: the union of the complete vertical line x=3 and complete
horizontal line y=-1. Their intersection (3,-1) is included.
Entire graph:
  {(3,t): t in R} union {(t,-1): t in R}.
Construction: use (3,0),(3,2) for the vertical line and (0,-1),(2,-1)
for the horizontal line; extend both lines fully and retain their crossing.
Completeness: these and only these pairs have a zero factor.

94. CONSTRUCT THE GRAPHS
Original subquestions: 2. Required results: 2 graphs.

94.a [PASS; required results 1]
Original: y=6/x.
Original domain: x!=0; also y!=0 because the numerator is nonzero.
Entire graph: {(t,6/t): t in R, t!=0}.
It is the hyperbola xy=6, with one branch in quadrant I and one in
quadrant III. The origin and both coordinate axes are absent.
Exact quadrant-I guide points:
  (1,6),(2,3),(3,2),(6,1).
Exact quadrant-III guide points:
  (-1,-6),(-2,-3),(-3,-2),(-6,-1).
Further central guide points are (sqrt(6),sqrt(6)) and its negative.
Asymptotes: x=0 and y=0; neither is part of the graph.
On each branch the function is decreasing as x increases:
for u<v with the same nonzero sign, 6/u-6/v=6(v-u)/(uv)>0.
End behavior:
  t -> 0+ gives y -> +infinity; t -> +infinity gives y -> 0+.
  t -> 0- gives y -> -infinity; t -> -infinity gives y -> 0-.
Construction: mark all guide points, then draw the smooth reciprocal branch
through the positive points with those two quadrant-I ends. Reflect that
entire branch through the origin to construct the quadrant-III branch.
Do not join the branches across x=0 or include a point on either axis.
The exact parameterization, not interpolation of guide points, defines
all intermediate points and proves that the full original graph is kept.

94.b [PASS; required results 1]
Original: xy=-12.
Since x=0 is impossible, equivalently y=-12/x with x!=0; y!=0 as well.
Entire graph: {(t,-12/t): t in R, t!=0}.
It is a hyperbola with branches in quadrants II and IV.
Exact quadrant-II guide points:
  (-12,1),(-6,2),(-4,3),(-3,4),(-2,6),(-1,12).
Exact quadrant-IV guide points:
  (1,-12),(2,-6),(3,-4),(4,-3),(6,-2),(12,-1).
Further central guide points:
  (-2*sqrt(3),2*sqrt(3)), (2*sqrt(3),-2*sqrt(3)).
Asymptotes: x=0 and y=0; no points on either axis are included.
On each branch the function is increasing as x increases:
for u<v with the same nonzero sign,
  (-12/v)-(-12/u)=12(v-u)/(uv)>0.
End behavior:
  t -> 0- gives y -> +infinity; t -> -infinity gives y -> 0+.
  t -> 0+ gives y -> -infinity; t -> +infinity gives y -> 0-.
Construction: draw the smooth quadrant-II reciprocal branch through its
guide points with the stated ends, then reflect through the origin for
the entire quadrant-IV branch. Do not cross or include the asymptotes.
The parameterization covers every permissible x and no extra solutions.

95. CONSTRUCT THE GRAPHS
Original subquestions: 6. Required results: 6 graphs.
For each positive radius below, (r*cos(theta),r*sin(theta)) with
0<=theta<2*pi denotes every point on the circumference exactly once.
Its squared distance is r^2; conversely any point of squared distance r^2
has that polar angle and appears in this range. This gives both directions
of the full-graph characterization. Each item has its own radius, points,
and concrete construction below.

95.a [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x^2+y^2=16.
Radius: sqrt(16)=4; center (0,0).
Entire graph: {(4*cos(theta),4*sin(theta)): 0<=theta<2*pi}.
Axis points: (4,0),(-4,0),(0,4),(0,-4).
Diagonal guide points: all four sign choices (2*sqrt(2),2*sqrt(2)).
Construction: set the compass opening to 4 coordinate units, center it
at the origin, and draw the whole circumference through the axis points.
No interior or exterior point belongs.

95.b [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x^2+y^2=36.
Radius: sqrt(36)=6; center (0,0).
Entire graph: {(6*cos(theta),6*sin(theta)): 0<=theta<2*pi}.
Axis points: (6,0),(-6,0),(0,6),(0,-6).
Diagonal guide points: all four sign choices (3*sqrt(2),3*sqrt(2)).
Construction: use a compass opening of 6 coordinate units and the origin
as center; draw the entire circumference through those four axis points.

95.v [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x^2+y^2=6 1/4, where 6 1/4 is the mixed number 25/4.
Radius: sqrt(25/4)=5/2; center (0,0).
Entire graph:
  {((5/2)*cos(theta),(5/2)*sin(theta)): 0<=theta<2*pi}.
Axis points: (5/2,0),(-5/2,0),(0,5/2),(0,-5/2).
Diagonal guide points: all four sign choices (5*sqrt(2)/4,5*sqrt(2)/4).
Construction: bisect a length of 5 coordinate units to obtain 5/2,
use that exact compass opening at the origin, and draw the full circle.
The mixed number is not interpreted as 6*(1/4).

95.g [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x^2+y^2=20.
Radius: sqrt(20)=2*sqrt(5); center (0,0).
Entire graph:
  {(2*sqrt(5)*cos(theta),2*sqrt(5)*sin(theta)): 0<=theta<2*pi}.
Axis points: (+/-2*sqrt(5),0),(0,+/-2*sqrt(5)).
Diagonal guide points: all four sign choices (sqrt(10),sqrt(10)).
Exact radius construction: construct a right triangle with perpendicular
legs of 4 and 2 coordinate units. Its hypotenuse has length
sqrt(4^2+2^2)=sqrt(20).
Transfer that hypotenuse length to a compass centered at the origin and
draw the entire circumference through the stated axis points.

95.d [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x^2+y^2=12.
Radius: sqrt(12)=2*sqrt(3); center (0,0).
Entire graph:
  {(2*sqrt(3)*cos(theta),2*sqrt(3)*sin(theta)): 0<=theta<2*pi}.
Axis points: (+/-2*sqrt(3),0),(0,+/-2*sqrt(3)).
Diagonal guide points: all four sign choices (sqrt(6),sqrt(6)).
Exact radius construction: first construct a square of side 2; its diagonal
is 2*sqrt(2). Use lengths 2 and that diagonal as the perpendicular legs
of a right triangle. Its hypotenuse has length
sqrt(2^2+(2*sqrt(2))^2)=sqrt(12).
Transfer that exact length to a compass at the origin and draw the entire
circumference, including every quadrant and all four axis points.

95.e [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x^2+y^2=0.
Both squares are nonnegative, so equality holds precisely when x=0,y=0.
Entire graph: {(0,0)}.
A degenerate parameterization is (0,0) for all 0<=theta<2*pi; it generates
only this one point, not a positive-radius circle.
Construction: mark a single point at the origin and nothing else.
Radius-zero language describes a degeneracy only; the positive-r circle
definition does not turn this graph into a circumference.

