99. EXPRESS x USING THE VARIABLE y
Original subquestions: 4. Required results: 4 complete relations.
Direction is x as a function of y, not y as a function of x.
For each original equation, define its y-fiber by
  F(y0)={x in R: (x,y0) satisfies the original equation}.
The formulas and exceptional fibers below specify the entire original
relation, including empty fibers rather than silently dropping cases.

99.a [PASS; required results 1]
Original: 2x+3y=7.
Rearrange: 2x=7-3y; divide by the nonzero constant 2.
Result: x=(7-3y)/2, for every real y.
Complete fibers: F(y)={(7-3y)/2} for all y in R.
Entire relation: {((7-3t)/2,t): t in R}.
Substitution gives 2*((7-3y)/2)+3y=7, proving sufficiency.
There are no exceptional or free-x fibers.

99.b [PASS; required results 1]
Original: 3x-5y=1.
Rearrange: 3x=1+5y; divide by the nonzero constant 3.
Result: x=(1+5y)/3, for every real y.
Complete fibers: F(y)={(1+5y)/3} for all y in R.
Entire relation: {((1+5t)/3,t): t in R}.
Substitution gives 3*((1+5y)/3)-5y=1.
There are no exceptional or free-x fibers.

99.v [PASS; required results 1]
Original: x+xy=10.
Factor: x(1+y)=10.
If y!=-1, division gives x=10/(1+y).
If y=-1, the ORIGINAL equation is x-x=10, hence 0=10; no x satisfies it.
Result: x=10/(1+y), y in R excluding -1.
Complete fibers:
  F(y)={10/(1+y)} when y!=-1;
  F(-1)=empty.
Entire relation: {(10/(1+t),t): t in R, t!=-1}.
For every allowed y, multiplication by 1+y recovers exactly 10.
No free-x fiber at y=-1 exists; no solution with x=0 exists.

99.g [PASS; required results 1]
Original: xy-y^2-2x=1.
Collect x terms: x(y-2)=y^2+1.
If y!=2, division gives x=(y^2+1)/(y-2).
If y=2, the ORIGINAL left side is 2x-4-2x=-4; the equation would
require -4=1, equivalently 0=5. No real x satisfies it.
Result: x=(y^2+1)/(y-2), y in R excluding 2.
Complete fibers:
  F(y)={(y^2+1)/(y-2)} when y!=2;
  F(2)=empty.
Entire relation: {((t^2+1)/(t-2),t): t in R, t!=2}.
Sufficiency: x(y-2)-y^2=(y^2+1)-y^2=1 for every allowed y.
The numerator y^2+1 is always positive, so x!=0; the y<2 part has
x<0 and the y>2 part has x>0. Both parts of the original relation are
retained. No arbitrary-x exceptional fiber is added.

100. SIMPLIFY WITH THE ORIGINAL EXPRESSION DOMAINS RETAINED
Original subquestions: 2. Required results: 2 domain-preserving expressions.

100.a [PASS; required results 1]
Original: (a^2+7a+6)/((a-3)^2-16).
Numerator: a^2+7a+6=(a+1)(a+6).
Denominator: (a-3)^2-16=((a-3)-4)((a-3)+4)=(a-7)(a+1).
Original real domain: a!=-1 and a!=7.
On this domain cancel the NONZERO factor a+1:
  (a^2+7a+6)/((a-3)^2-16)=(a+6)/(a-7).
Result: (a+6)/(a-7), with a in R excluding {-1,7}.
The restriction a!=-1 remains even though the reduced displayed
denominator no longer shows it. The original expression is undefined
there; the reduced unrestricted expression would give -5/8.
Thus the removable hole at (-1,-5/8), when viewed as a function graph,
is not filled. The other forbidden value a=7 also remains forbidden.

100.b [PASS; required results 1]
Original: (4b^2-7b-2)/(81*(b+7)^2).
Numerator: 4b^2-7b-2=(4b+1)(b-2).
Original real domain: b!=-7.
At b=-7 the numerator is 4*49+49-2=243, not 0, so it has no
factor b+7. There is no polynomial common factor to cancel.
Result: ((4b+1)(b-2))/(81*(b+7)^2), with b in R excluding {-7}.
Expansion of the retained numerator recovers 4b^2-7b-2.
No extra numerator-based domain restrictions are imposed; b=2 and
b=-1/4 remain allowed and give zero.

101. SOLVE THE REAL INEQUALITIES WITH STRICT BOUNDARIES
Original subquestions: 4. Required results: 4 real solution sets.

101.a [PASS; required results 1]
Original: P^2-10P+21<0.
Factor: (P-3)(P-7)<0.
For P<3 both factors are negative, giving a positive product.
For 3<P<7 the factors have opposite signs, giving a negative product.
For P>7 both factors are positive, giving a positive product.
At P=3 or P=7 the product is zero and fails the strict inequality.
Result: P in (3,7), both endpoints excluded.

101.b [PASS; required results 1]
Original: u^2-8u+16>0.
Complete square: (u-4)^2>0.
For real u this is positive precisely when u!=4; at u=4 it is zero.
Result: u in (-infinity,4) union (4,+infinity).
The boundary value 4 is strictly excluded.

101.v [PASS; required results 1]
Original: q^2+6q+13>0.
Complete square: (q+3)^2+4>0.
For every real q the left side is at least 4>0; its minimum is
attained at q=-3 and is still strictly positive.
Result: q in R. There are no real boundary roots to exclude.

101.g [PASS; required results 1]
